---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import MoreTools from '../components/MoreTools.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import { site } from '../site.config';
import { withBase } from '../lib/url';

const title = `About ${site.name}`;
const description = `How ${site.name} converts, compresses and resizes images locally in your browser: methodology, libraries and licences, and how to verify nothing is uploaded.`;
const updated = site.launched;
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="container container--narrow section prose">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'About', url: '/about/' }]} />
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="muted small">Last updated: {updated}</p>
    <p>{site.description}</p>

    <h2>Methodology</h2>
    <h3>Decoding</h3>
    <p>
      Most formats (JPG, PNG, WebP, GIF, BMP, and AVIF where the browser supports it) are decoded with the browser's native{' '}
      <code>createImageBitmap()</code>, using <code>imageOrientation: 'from-image'</code> so a photo's EXIF rotation flag is
      baked into the pixels before any further processing — this is what keeps a portrait photo upright after its metadata
      is stripped. HEIC/HEIF files are decoded with{' '}
      <a href="https://github.com/strukturag/libheif" rel="noopener">libheif</a>, compiled to WebAssembly by the{' '}
      <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/libheif-js" rel="noopener">libheif-js</a> package, loaded only when a HEIC
      file is actually added (a separate, lazily-fetched script chunk).
    </p>
    <h3>Resizing</h3>
    <p>
      A "max width/height" resize scales the image down to fit inside the given box without cropping (never upscales). A
      percentage resize scales both dimensions by that factor. A preset (social square, OG image, passport size) crops to
      the target aspect ratio around the image's centre, then scales to the exact pixel size — see{' '}
      <code>src/lib/resize.ts</code>.
    </p>
    <h3>Compression to an exact size</h3>
    <p>
      For "compress to N KB", Pixlite binary-searches the JPG/WebP quality parameter (0.01–0.95) — typically 6–8 encode
      attempts — until the resulting file is at or under the target; the target is treated as a hard maximum, never an
      approximate one. If even the lowest quality setting still produces a file larger than the target, the image is scaled
      down by ~18% and the quality search runs again, up to 4 times, before reporting the best size actually achieved (with
      a "best effort" badge, since that file is still over target). The search algorithm is a pure, unit-tested function (
      <code>src/lib/target-size.ts</code>) decoupled from the encoder, so its behaviour is deterministic and verifiable.
      PNG is excluded because it is lossless — there is no quality dial to search over.
    </p>
    <h3>Metadata removal</h3>
    <p>
      Re-encoding an image via the Canvas API only ever copies pixel data — EXIF, IPTC, XMP and GPS blocks are not part of
      that pixel data, so they are dropped automatically on every export, with no separate "strip" step to forget. Before
      export, EXIF is read locally (not uploaded) with{' '}
      <a href="https://github.com/MikeKovarik/exifr" rel="noopener">exifr</a> purely to warn you if a GPS location is
      present.
    </p>

    <h2>Libraries used and their licences</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Library</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Licence</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>libheif-js</td><td>HEIC/HEIF decoding (WASM), loaded only when needed</td><td>LGPL-3.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>exifr</td><td>Local EXIF/GPS metadata reading</td><td>MIT</td></tr>
          <tr><td>jszip</td><td>Client-side ZIP export</td><td>MIT</td></tr>
          <tr><td>satori</td><td>Build-time Open Graph image generation</td><td>MIT</td></tr>
          <tr><td>sharp</td><td>Build-time icon/OG raster output (never runs in the browser)</td><td>Apache-2.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Preact</td><td>Workbench UI island</td><td>MIT</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Astro</td><td>Static site framework</td><td>MIT</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="muted small">
      libheif-js is LGPL-3.0 and is used unmodified, loaded dynamically as its own script chunk — it is not statically
      linked into the site's main bundle. Its source is available at{' '}
      <a href="https://github.com/catdad-experiments/libheif-js" rel="noopener">github.com/catdad-experiments/libheif-js</a>,
      and the full licence text is at{' '}
      <a href="https://www.gnu.org/licenses/lgpl-3.0.txt" rel="noopener">gnu.org/licenses/lgpl-3.0.txt</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Format facts sources</h2>
    <p>
      The per-format comparison tables (transparency, animation, lossless/lossy, browser support) on tool and guide pages
      reflect the published specifications and browser compatibility data from{' '}
      <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Media/Formats/Image_types" rel="noopener">MDN Web Docs — Image file type and format guide</a>{' '}
      and the format owners' own documentation (<a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/PNG/" rel="noopener">W3C PNG spec</a>,{' '}
      <a href="https://developers.google.com/speed/webp" rel="noopener">Google's WebP documentation</a>,{' '}
      <a href="https://nokiatech.github.io/heif/" rel="noopener">HEIF format documentation</a>), current as of {updated}.
    </p>

    <h2>Known limits (stated honestly)</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>AVIF encoding is not offered (browsers cannot encode AVIF from a canvas yet) — AVIF can be decoded and converted away from, not to.</li>
      <li>GIF input keeps only the first frame; there is no animated GIF/WebP output.</li>
      <li>Very large batches (100+ full-resolution photos) on an older phone will be slower — files over {site.limits.maxLongestSidePx}px on the longest side are flagged so you know why.</li>
      <li>The passport-size tool resizes to standard pixel dimensions only; it does not check background colour, head position or other biometric rules — verify those separately with your issuing office.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Monetization</h2>
    <p>
      Pixlite is free. It may show non-intrusive, clearly-labelled ad slots below the workbench (never over the tool
      itself) and, in future, affiliate links to cloud storage or photo-printing services — see <code>PRODUCT.md</code> in
      the source repository for the exact plan. No affiliate link is live yet.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy by design</h2>
    <p>
      {site.name} runs entirely in your browser. Files are not uploaded, no account is needed, and there are no tracking
      cookies. See the <a href={withBase('/privacy-proof/')}>privacy proof page</a> to verify this yourself, and the{' '}
      <a href={withBase('/privacy/')}>privacy policy</a> for the full detail.
    </p>

    <h2>Who maintains it</h2>
    <p>
      Built and maintained by <a href={site.author.url} rel="noopener">{site.author.name}</a>. Launched {site.launched}.
      Found a bug, a wrong format fact, or have a suggestion? <a href={withBase('/contact/')}>Get in touch</a>.
    </p>
  </article>

  <Fragment slot="footer">
    <MoreTools />
    <SiteFooter />
  </Fragment>
</Base>
